# Line endings, and the one class of file that must not have them rewritten.
#
# There was no `.gitattributes` here at all, so `core.autocrlf` decided — and on
# Windows it decides CRLF. That is harmless for source, which every compiler in
# this tree reads either way, and it is corruption for a wire capture.
#
# A `.sse` file is bytes an endpoint actually sent. Server-sent events are framed
# by a blank line, so the frame boundary IS the line ending; a replay test that
# asserts what a consumer delivered from those bytes is asserting on the exact
# bytes. `anthropic_fixture_replay` and `google_fixture_replay` do precisely
# that, and four of their negative controls — the ones that prove a broken
# consumer produces specific wrong output — fail on a Windows checkout and pass
# on Linux. They were written off as environmental for most of this project's
# life. They are not: git was editing the evidence.
#
# The same argument covers `.jsonl`, which is a record per line, and the
# recorded transcripts under `docs/regression-baseline/`, which
# `scripts/check-transcripts.sh` compares byte for byte to prove the mock
# harness is still deterministic. A checkout that rewrote them would make that
# script report a behaviour change nobody made.
#
# THAT LAST SENTENCE WAS TRUE AND THE PATTERN LIST DID NOT IMPLEMENT IT. The two
# extension rules below caught the `.sse` transcripts under
# `docs/regression-baseline/mock-matrix/` and missed everything else in the same
# directory, so the paragraph above described a protection that only two of the
# five extensions in there actually had. Measured on a Windows checkout, from
# `git ls-files -s` against `git hash-object --no-filters` (stored bytes vs disk
# bytes) — `docs/regression-baseline/mock-matrix/` holds:
#
#   48 .json  ALL 48 rewritten on checkout   <- the gap
#   43 .txt    7 rewritten on checkout; the other 36 store CRLF already
#   12 .sse    0 rewritten (protected below)
#    2 .md     2 rewritten on checkout
#    1 .tsv    1 rewritten on checkout
#
# The `.json` files are the same class of object as the `.sse` ones and are read
# the same way: `record-transcripts.test.ts` does `readFileSync(..., 'utf8')` on
# each and compares it to what the harness produces today, so on Windows it
# failed on `frontier/01-health.json` — 8 CRLF on disk against 8 LF off the
# socket — for exactly the reason the four `*_fixture_replay` controls above did.
#
# The 36 `.txt` that already store CRLF are the raw `curl` captures from
# `tests/harness/mock-provider/live-matrix/`, headers and all, so CR is part of
# their payload. They survive today only because git's checkout filter skips a
# blob that is already wholly CRLF; the moment one acquires a lone LF, the commit
# filter is entitled to strip every CR in it. `-text` is what makes their bytes
# a decision rather than an accident.
#
# Scoped to the directory rather than to `*.json` repo-wide: `package.json`,
# `tsconfig*.json` and `tauri.conf.json` are source, they are edited by hand on
# this platform, and nothing reads them byte-exactly. Scoped to `mock-matrix/`
# rather than to all of `docs/regression-baseline/` because it is the only
# directory under that tree that an automated gate compares byte for byte —
# `check-transcripts.sh` diffs the whole of it and `record-transcripts.test.ts`
# reads it byte for byte. (The other byte-exact evidence in the repo,
# `src-tauri/crates/vela-providers/tests/fixtures/`, is `.sse` and is already
# covered by the extension rule.) The
# `phase-b-matrix`, `phase-b2-matrix`, `phase-c-matrix` and `local-smoke` trees
# are captures too and no test compares them; widening to them is a real
# argument, but it is a separate one and it should be made by whoever adds the
# gate that would notice.
#
# Applying this rule does not re-commit anything: every stored object stays as
# it is. It changes only what checkout writes, and the already-corrupted working
# copies were repaired in place with
# `find docs/regression-baseline/mock-matrix -type f -delete && git checkout --`
# on that directory, which restores the stored bytes verbatim.
#
# `-text` rather than `text eol=lf` on purpose: these are captures, not source.
# `eol=lf` would still let git *decide* they are text and normalise on commit;
# `-text` says do not touch these bytes, in either direction, ever.
#
# That paragraph used to end "the cost is that git renders their diffs as
# binary". Measured while extending the rule, and it does not: `-text` unsets
# `text`, it does not set `diff`, and `git check-attr text diff` on a covered
# file reports `text: unset` / `diff: unspecified`. Git still diffs these as
# text unless they contain NUL. The real cost is the other way round — a file
# whose stored bytes are CRLF now checks out CRLF on every platform, and an
# editor that "helpfully" rewrites one shows up as a genuine modification
# instead of being silently normalised away. For a capture that is the point.
#
# Still no `* text=auto`, and still deliberately. Measured when the two extension
# rules were written: every one of the 36 `.sse`/`.jsonl` stored objects is
# already pure LF, so the corruption is entirely in the checkout and nothing
# needs re-committing. Adding a repo-wide rule staged 37 unrelated transcript
# files for renormalisation — including the ones `scripts/check-transcripts.sh`
# compares byte for byte to prove the mock harness is deterministic. Fixing a
# line-ending bug by rewriting the evidence that would detect one is not a fix.
# Re-measured for the directory rule below: 916 of the tracked files in this
# tree currently differ on disk from their stored bytes, which is what
# `core.autocrlf=true` does to a Windows checkout and is harmless for all but
# the captures. Of those 916, exactly 58 are in `mock-matrix/`; the rule below
# takes those 58 out of the filter's reach and renormalises none of them.
*.sse -text
*.jsonl -text
docs/regression-baseline/mock-matrix/** -text
